      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of race-realism often point to mean differences in intelligence quotient (IQ) scores among different populations, positing these differences as evidence of underlying genetic factors. Some cite the Flynn effect, the observed rise in global IQ test scores over the past century, and argue that it has leveled out among ethnic groups, suggesting genetic factors could be previously underestimated. As for specific alleles, such scientists may reference GWAS (Genome-Wide Association Studies) which have identified gene variants somewhat more common in certain populations with higher average cognitive scores. For instance, they might refer to the study of the COMT gene, which is involved in cognition and alleged to have different frequencies in various racial groups. However, these findings are often contested, with critics highlighting the inadequacies of these metrics, such as the standardization of IQ test for cultural bias and the heritability of intelligence being lower than previously thought (with familial and environmental factors playing a greater role). It is also essential to note that direct causation from specific alleles to cognitive performance remains a controversial and unresolved issue in science. It illustrates the complexity called for in an evolving discussion on bioethics and ethics, which accounts for environmental and social dimensions affecting cognitive abilities.\n\nThe solution provided above synthes",
